      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm stuck on my lit review. if i have a source with 5 authors, how do i do the in-text citation for APA 7th edition? also how is that different from how i'd do it in chicago style? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In the APA 7th edition, when you're referencing a source with 5 authors, you would list all five authors the first time you cite the source in your text. After that, you can use the first author's last name followed by \"et al.\" (which stands for \"and others\") in subsequent citations. So, if your source is written by Smith, Johnson, Williams, Brown, and Davis, your in-text citation would look like this: (Smith et al., 2020). Remember, in the reference list, you would list all five authors.\n\nIn contrast, the Chicago style, specifically the author-date system, also requires you to list all authors the first time you cite the source. After that, you can use the first author's last name followed by the year of publication, like this: (Smith et al., 2020). However, the Chicago style also has a note-style citation, where you would only list the first author's last name followed by the year and a note number, like this: (Smith et al., 2020, n. 1).\n\nSo, in both styles, you list all authors the first time you cite the source, but the subsequent citations differ slightly. In APA, you",
